6 Portable node labels
A conservative label subset works across most phylogenetic software:

lowercase and uppercase letters;
numerals;
periods; and
underscores.

(Agkistrodon_contortrix,Agkistrodon.piscivorus);
The original Newick convention interprets an underscore in an unquoted label as a blank. Some software preserves the underscore instead. Normalize labels deliberately when their exact spelling must survive a multi-program workflow.

8 Newick data files
A Newick data file contains 1 or more terminated Newick expressions:
(A,(B,(C,D)));
(D,(A,(B,C)));
(A,(C,(D,B)));
The semicolon terminates each expression. Placing 1 expression on each line is a readable convention, not a format requirement. Outside an unquoted label or branch length, whitespace and line breaks may separate syntax elements.
NEXUS trees blocks also use Newick expressions, but NEXUS adds its own block structure and statements around those expressions.

3 Resolve organism concepts with NCBI Taxonomy
Common names do not define exact database targets. A search for bears can include extant species, extinct species, subspecies, environmental samples, and records identified only to genus.
The NCBI Taxonomy Browser shows the accepted name, rank, lineage, and Taxonomy ID for a name. The browser resolves bears to Ursidae, Taxonomy ID 9632, at the rank family.
The following Entrez query selects the specified, non-extinct species beneath Ursidae:
txid9632[subtree] AND species[rank] AND specified[prop] NOT extinct[prop]







Query term
Selection




txid9632[subtree]
Ursidae and all descendants.


species[rank]
Taxa assigned the species rank.


specified[prop]
Taxa identified more precisely than Ursus sp. or an environmental sample.


NOT extinct[prop]
Taxa without the NCBI extinct property.



NCBI Taxonomy supplies the names and classification used by NCBI sequence records. NCBI states that its taxonomy is not an authoritative nomenclatural source or a phylogenetic hypothesis. The NCBI Taxonomy documentation describes its scope.

